\vspace{-2pt}
\subsection{Synthesis: Training-Associated vs.\ Inherited Routing}
\label{sec:routing_synthesis}

\looseness=-1
The three analyses separate two patterns: exit collapse and counterfactual sensitivity are training-associated (pronounced in VLM-to-VLA, small in VLA-to-VLA), while L1 attention is inherited from reference backbones.
Despite different reference starting points, trained OpenVLA and TraceVLA converge to nearly identical mid-layer peaks ($R^2 = 0.637$ at L9 vs.\ $0.634$ at L11, $\Delta = 0.003$; Appendix~\ref{sec:cross_arch_r2}), suggesting convergence toward a shared mid-layer structure.
These probes measure decodability, not causal reliance (label-permutation controls in Appendix~\ref{sec:label_permutation}).
Matched-size ablation reveals that instruction sensitivity is distributed across heads in proportion to their instruction attention fraction ($d = 0.65$ vs.\ $0.60$, 8\% gap; Appendix Table~\ref{tab:specificity_control}), consistent with instruction information being woven into the residual stream rather than concentrated in a dedicated circuit.
The full 32-layer attention profile (Figure~\ref{fig:synthesis_panels}, left) reveals three qualitatively distinct patterns: VLA-trained shows an entry-exit concentration (L1: 24, L31: 18 instruction-dominant heads), Llama-2 base peaks at mid-depth (L16: 25 heads), and random initialization maintains 0 heads at all layers.
Dose-response ablation of L1 instruction-dominant heads (Figure~\ref{fig:synthesis_panels}, right) yields monotonic trajectory divergence ($d = 0.48$--$0.64$), providing causal evidence that L1 gating contributes to downstream action computation.

