\section{Discussion and Conclusion}
\label{sec:discussion}
\vspace{-2pt}

\paragraph{Limitations.}\looseness=-1
Our conclusions rest on $N{=}2$ VLM-to-VLA transitions plus one VLA-to-VLA case, all causal-decoder architectures on a single benchmark (LIBERO); flow-matching~\cite{black2024_pi0} and diffusion~\cite{ghosh2024_octo} action heads may differ.
All findings are correlational: probe $R^2$ reflects decodability, not causal reliance.
The $\Delta R^2$--success-rate correlation ($\rho{=}0.583$, $p{=}0.10$; Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_discussion_appendix}) is underpowered ($n{=}9$); each architecture uses a single checkpoint; the three-tier gradient has $n{=}1$ at Tier~2.
All analyses are open-loop~\cite{grant2026,haon2025}; closed-loop validation and intermediate-checkpoint dynamics are priorities for future work.

\paragraph{Conclusion.}\looseness=-1
We present a cross-architecture decomposition of instruction routing in VLAs, identifying entry gating, mid-layer integration, and exit collapse across three architectures, two training pathways, and four instruction conditions.
$\Delta R^2 > 0.10$ provides a probe-based screening signal supplementing rollout evaluation; mid-layer features outperform exit-layer representations where collapse is pronounced (Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_practical_implications}). Code and data will be released upon acceptance.

